1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,671
You move a file into place, and the service still cannot read it.

2
00:00:04,691 --> 00:00:10,151
The file carried its old SELinux label with it. The fix is one command: restorecon.

3
00:00:10,621 --> 00:00:13,438
In this video: restore default file contexts.

4
00:00:17,958 --> 00:00:20,541
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:21,011 --> 00:00:22,953
Restore default file contexts.

6
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:27,623
We'll make a file with a wrong label, look up the default, and restore it:

7
00:00:27,643 --> 00:00:29,505
one file, then a whole directory.

8
00:00:37,025 --> 00:00:38,573
Why does a label go wrong?

9
00:00:38,593 --> 00:00:42,130
A new file gets the label of the place where it is created.

10
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:45,567
But when you move a file with mv, it keeps its old label,

11
00:00:45,587 --> 00:00:48,099
even in a place where that label is wrong.

12
00:00:48,119 --> 00:00:51,234
The same happens with a file restored from a backup.

13
00:00:56,704 --> 00:00:58,247
The cure is restorecon.

14
00:00:58,267 --> 00:01:02,459
It looks up the default context in the policy, and applies it.

15
00:01:02,479 --> 00:01:05,176
The option dash v shows what it changes.

16
00:01:05,196 --> 00:01:08,844
The option dash capital R does a whole directory tree.

17
00:01:17,364 --> 00:01:19,666
First, a file with a wrong label.

18
00:01:19,686 --> 00:01:23,747
We write a line into note dot t x t in the home directory.

19
00:01:23,767 --> 00:01:28,039
The greater-than sign sends the output of echo into the file.

20
00:01:28,059 --> 00:01:31,839
Its type is user home t, correct for a home directory.

21
00:01:31,859 --> 00:01:37,186
Now mv moves it into slash etc. And the label came along: still user home t.

22
00:01:37,206 --> 00:01:41,408
In slash etc, the default is etc t. This file is mislabeled.

23
00:01:45,928 --> 00:01:50,389
How do we know the default? matchpathcon prints it, from the policy:

24
00:01:50,409 --> 00:01:54,079
for this path, etc t. It only shows, it changes nothing.

25
00:01:57,599 --> 00:02:01,990
Now restore it. restorecon with dash v says what it did:

26
00:02:02,010 --> 00:02:07,819
relabeled, from user home t, to etc t. And ls confirms: the type is etc t.

27
00:02:11,339 --> 00:02:12,999
Now a whole directory.

28
00:02:13,019 --> 00:02:18,956
mkdir makes the directory site, and touch creates an empty file index dot html

29
00:02:18,976 --> 00:02:22,621
inside it. We move the directory into slash srv.

30
00:02:22,641 --> 00:02:27,127
Both the directory and the file keep the label user home t.

31
00:02:30,647 --> 00:02:35,845
restorecon with dash capital R and dash v goes through the whole tree.

32
00:02:35,865 --> 00:02:41,435
It relabels the directory and the file. The default for slash srv is var t.

33
00:02:41,455 --> 00:02:43,000
And ls confirms both.

34
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:53,040
Clean up. rm removes the file, and rm with dash r removes the directory and

35
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:56,439
everything inside it. The system is as it was.

36
00:03:00,959 --> 00:03:05,760
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot when the task needs one, and your turn.

37
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:12,530
Your turn. The setup script puts a file named practice dot conf in slash etc,

38
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:13,830
with a wrong label.

39
00:03:13,850 --> 00:03:17,183
Run it, look at the label with ls dash capital Z,

40
00:03:17,203 --> 00:03:21,084
restore the default with restorecon, and run the checker.

41
00:03:31,554 --> 00:03:33,305
Try before you open the solution.

42
00:03:38,825 --> 00:03:41,536
Next up: manage SELinux port labels.

43
00:03:42,006 --> 00:03:44,908
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
